"Sumcheck API Documentation | ICICLE Docs (https://dev.ingonyama.com/api/cpp/sumcheck)\nciteturn6view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://dev.ingonyama.com/api/cpp/sumcheck\",\"pattern\":\"extension\"}); Total lines: 246\nL48:     * 【39†Rust Overview】\nL49:     * 【40†MSM】\nL50:     * 【41†NTT】\nL51:     * 【42†ECNTT】\nL52:     * 【43†Vector operations】\nL53:     * 【44†Program】\nL54:     * 【45†Polynomials】\nL55:     * 【46†Hash】\nL56:     * 【47†Merkle-Tree】\nL57:     * 【48†Sumcheck】\nL58:     * 【49†FRI】\nL59:     * 【50†Lattice SNARKs】\nL60:     * 【51†PQC ML-KEM】\nL61:     * 【52†Serialization】\nL62:     * 【53†Pairings】\nL63:     * 【54†Matrix Operations】\nL64: \nL65: Version: 4.0.0\nL66: \nL67: [Button: On this page]\nL68: # Sumcheck API Documentation\nL69: ## Overview【55†​】\nL70: \nL71: The Sumcheck protocol allows a Prover to prove to a Verifier that the sum of a multilinear polynomial, over the Boolean hypercube, equals a specific scalar value.\nL72: \nL73: For a polynomial $P$P with $n$n variables, the Prover aims to prove that:\nL74: \nL75: $$\\sum_{X_1 \\in \\{0, 1\\}}\\sum_{X_2 \\in \\{0, 1\\}}\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot\\sum_{X_n \\in \\{0, 1\\}} P(X_1, X_2,..., X_n) = C,$$X 1​∈{0,1}∑​X 2​∈{0,1}∑​⋅⋅⋅X n​∈{0,1}∑​P(X 1​,X 2​,...,X n​)=C,\nL76: \nL77: where $C$C is some scalar.\nL78: The proof is constructed interactively, involving a series of $n$n rounds. Each round consists of a challenge (from the Verifier), a computation (from the Prover), and a verification step (by the Verifier). Using a Fiat-Shamir (FS) scheme, the proof becomes non-interactive, enabling the Prover to generate the entire proof and send it to the Verifier for validation.\nL79: ### Sumcheck with Combine Function【56†​】\nL80: \nL81: The Sumcheck protocol can be generalized to handle multiple multilinear polynomials. In this case, assuming there are $m$m polynomials of $n$n variables, and $f$f is an arbitrary function that takes these $m$m polynomials and returns a polynomial of equal or higher degree, the Prover tries to prove that:\nL82: $$\\sum_{X_1 \\in \\{0, 1\\}}\\sum_{X_2 \\in \\{0, 1\\}}\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot\\sum_{X_n \\in \\{0, 1\\}} f\\left(P_1(X_1, ..., X_n), P_2(X_1, ..., X_n), ..., P_m(X_1, ..., X_n)\\right) = C,$$X 1​∈{0,1}∑​X 2​∈{0,1}∑​⋅⋅⋅X n​∈{0,1}∑​f(P 1​(X 1​,...,X n​),P 2​(X 1​,...,X n​),...,P m​(X 1​,...,X n​))=C,\nL83: \nL84: where $C$C is some scalar.\nL85: ## ICICLE's Sumecheck Implementation【57†​】\nL86: \nL87: ICICLE implements a non-interactive Sumcheck protocol that supports a combine function. It is available on both the CPU and CUDA backends of ICICLE. The polynomials are passed to the protocol in MLE (evaluation representation) form.\nL88: ### Implementation Limitations【58†​】\nL89: \nL90: There are some limitations and assumptions in the Sumcheck implementation:\nL91:   * The maximum size of the polynomials (i.e., the number of evaluations $2^n$2 n) depends on the number of polynomials and the memory size of the device (e.g., CPU/GPU) being used. For example, with 4 polynomials, a GPU with 24GB of memory should be able to handle Sumcheck for polynomials of size up to $2^29$2 2 9.\nL92:   * The polynomial size must a power of 2.\nL93:   * The current implementation does not support generating the challenge ($\\alpha$α) in an extension field. This functionality is necessary for ensuring security when working with small fields.\nL94: ## C++ API【59†​】\nL95: \nL96: A Sumcheck object can be created using the following function:\nL97: \nL98:     `Sumcheck<scalar_t> create_sumcheck()\nL99:     `\nL100: \nL101: There are two key configuration structs related to the Sumcheck protocol.\nL102: ### SumcheckConfig【60†​】\nL103: \nL104: The `SumcheckConfig` struct is used to specify parameters for the Sumcheck protocol. It contains the following fields:\nL105:   * `stream: icicleStreamHandle`: The CUDA stream for asynchronous execution. If `nullptr`, the default stream is used.\nL106:   * `use_extension_field: bool`: If true, an extension field is used for Fiat-Shamir results. Currently unsupported (should always be false).\nL107:   * `are_inputs_on_device: bool`: If true, the input polynomials are expected to reside on the device (e.g., GPU); otherwise, they are expected to reside on the host (e.g., CPU).\nL108:   * `is_async: bool`: If true runs the hash asynchronously.\nL109:   * `ext: ConfigExtension*`: Backend-specific extensions.\nL110: The default values are:\nL111: \nL112:     `  struct SumcheckConfig {\nL113:         icicleStreamHandle stream = nullptr;\nL114:         bool use_extension_field = false;\nL115:         uint64_t batch = 1;\nL116:         bool are_inputs_on_device =false;\nL117:         bool is_async = false;\nL118:         ConfigExtension* ext = nullptr;\nL119:       };\nL120:     `\nL121: ### SumcheckTranscriptConfig【61†​】\nL122: \nL123: The `SumcheckTranscriptConfig<F>` class is used to specify parameters for the Fiat-Shamir scheme used by the Sumcheck protocol. It contains the following fields:\nL124:   * `hasher: Hash`: The hash function used to generate randomness for Fiat-Shamir.\nL125:   * `domain_label: char*`: The label for the domain separator in the transcript.\nL126:   * `poly_label: char*`: The label for round polynomials in the transcript.\nL127:   * `challenge_label: char*`: The label for round challenges in the transcript.\nL128:   * `seed: F`: The seed for initializing the RNG.\nL129:   * `little_endian: bool`: The encoding endianness.\nL130: There are three constructors for `SumcheckTranscriptConfig<F>`, each with its own default values:\nL131: \nL132:   * Default constructor:\nL133: \nL134:     `    SumcheckTranscriptConfig()\nL135:             : m_little_endian(true), m_seed_rng(F::from(0)), m_hasher(std::move(create_keccak_256_hash()))\nL136:     `\nL137:   * Constructor with byte vector for labels:\nL138: \nL139:     `    SumcheckTranscriptConfig(\nL140:           Hash hasher,\nL141:           std::vector<std::byte>&& domain_label,\nL142:           std::vector<std::byte>&& poly_label,\nL143:           std::vector<std::byte>&& challenge_label,\nL144:           F seed,\nL145:           bool little_endian = true)\nL146:             : m_hasher(std::move(hasher)), m_domain_separator_label(domain_label), m_round_poly_label(poly_label),\nL147:               m_round_challenge_label(challenge_label), m_little_endian(little_endian), m_seed_rng(seed)\nL148:     `\nL149:   * Constructor with `const char*` arguments for labels:\nL150: \nL151:     `    SumcheckTranscriptConfig(\nL152:           Hash hasher,\nL153:           const char* domain_label,\nL154:           const char* poly_label,\nL155:           const char* challenge_label,\nL156:           F seed,\nL157:           bool little_endian = true)\nL158:             : m_hasher(std::move(hasher)), m_domain_separator_label(cstr_to_bytes(domain_label)),\nL159:               m_round_poly_label(cstr_to_bytes(poly_label)), m_round_challenge_label(cstr_to_bytes(challenge_label)),\nL160:               m_little_endian(little_endian), m_seed_rng(seed)\nL161:     `\nL162: ### Generating Sumcheck Proofs【62†​】\nL163: \nL164: To generate a proof, first, an empty proof needs to be created. The Sumcheck proof is represented by the `SumcheckProof<S>` class:\nL165: \nL166:     `template <typename S>\nL167:     class SumcheckProof\nL168:     `\nL169: \nL170: The class has a default constructor `SumcheckProof()` that takes no arguments.\nL171: The proof can be generated using the get_proof method from the `Sumcheck<F>` object:\nL172: \nL173:     `eIcicleError get_proof(\nL174:       const std::vector<F*>& mle_polynomials,\nL175:       const uint64_t mle_polynomial_size,\nL176:       const F& claimed_sum,\nL177:       const ReturningValueProgram<F>& combine_function,\nL178:       const SumcheckTranscriptConfig<F>&& transcript_config,\nL179:       const SumcheckConfig& sumcheck_config,\nL180:       SumcheckProof<F>& sumcheck_proof /*out*/) const\nL181:     `\nL182: \nL183: The arguments for this method are:\nL184:   * `mle_polynomials: std::vector<F*>&`: A vector of pointers, each pointing to the evaluations of one of the input polynomials.\nL185:   * `mle_polynomial_size: uint64_t`: The length of the polynomials (number of evaluations). This should be a power of 2.\nL186:   * `claimed_sum: F&`: The sum the Prover claims to be the sum of the combine function over the evaluations of the polynomials.\nL187:   * `combine_function: ReturningValueProgram<F>&`: The combine function, using ICICLE's 【20†program】 API.\nL188:   * `transcript_config: SumcheckTranscriptConfig<F>&&`: The configuration for the Fiat-Shamir scheme.\nL189:   * `sumcheck_config: SumcheckConfig&`: The configuration for the Sumcheck protocol.\nL190:   * `sumcheck_proof: SumcheckProof<F>&`: The output `SumcheckProof` object containing the generated proof.\nL191: #### Example: Generating a Proof【63†​】\nL192: \nL193:     `auto prover_sumcheck = create_sumcheck<scalar_t>();\nL194:     SumcheckTranscriptConfig<scalar_t> transcript_config; // default configuration\nL195:     ReturningValueProgram<scalar_t> combine_func(EQ_X_AB_MINUS_C);\nL196:     SumcheckConfig sumcheck_config;\nL197:     SumcheckProof<scalar_t> sumcheck_proof;\nL198:     ICICLE_CHECK(prover_sumcheck.get_proof(\nL199:       mle_polynomials, mle_poly_size, claimed_sum, combine_func, std::move(transcript_config), sumcheck_config,\nL200:       sumcheck_proof));\nL201:     `\nL202: ### Verifying Sumcheck Proofs【64†​】\nL203: \nL204: To verify the proof, the Verifier should use the verify method of the `Sumcheck<F>` object:\nL205: \nL206:     `eIcicleError verify(\nL207:       const SumcheckProof<F>& sumcheck_proof,\nL208:       const F& claimed_sum,\nL209:       const SumcheckTranscriptConfig<F>&& transcript_config,\nL210:       bool& valid /*out*/)\nL211:     `\nL212: \nL213: The arguments for this method are:\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[1704.02086] A Zero Knowledge Sumcheck and its Applications (https://arxiv.org/abs/1704.02086)\nciteturn6view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1704.02086\",\"pattern\":\"Submission history\"}); Total lines: 175\nL9: # Title:A Zero Knowledge Sumcheck and its Applications\nL10: \nL16: > Abstract:Many seminal results in Interactive Proofs (IPs) use algebraic techniques based on low-degree polynomials, the study of which is pervasive in theoretical computer science. Unfortunately, known methods for endowing such proofs with zero knowledge guarantees do not retain this rich algebraic structure.\nL17: > In this work, we develop algebraic techniques for obtaining zero knowledge variants of proof protocols in a way that leverages and preserves their algebraic structure. Our constructions achieve unconditional (perfect) zero knowledge in the Interactive Probabilistically Checkable Proof (IPCP) model of Kalai and Raz [KR08] (the prover first sends a PCP oracle, then the prover and verifier engage in an Interactive Proof in which the verifier may query the PCP).\nL18: > Our main result is a zero knowledge variant of the sumcheck protocol [LFKN92] in the IPCP model. The sumcheck protocol is a key building block in many IPs, including the protocol for polynomial-space computation due to Shamir [Sha92], and the protocol for parallel computation due to Goldwasser, Kalai, and Rothblum [GKR15]. A core component of our result is an algebraic commitment scheme, whose hiding property is guaranteed by algebraic query complexity lower bounds [AW09,JKRS09].\nL19: This commitment scheme can then be used to considerably strengthen our previous work [BCFGRS16] that gives a sumcheck protocol with much weaker zero knowledge guarantees, itself using algebraic techniques based on algorithms for polynomial identity testing [RS05,BW04].\nL20: > We demonstrate the applicability of our techniques by deriving zero knowledge variants of well-known protocols based on algebraic techniques, including the protocols of Shamir and of Goldwasser, Kalai, and Rothblum, as well as the protocol of Babai, Fortnow, and Lund [BFL91].\nL21: Subjects:  | Computational Complexity (cs.CC); Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)\nL22: Cite as:  | 【10†arXiv:1704.02086】 [cs.CC]\nL23:    | (or 【11†arXiv:1704.02086v1】 [cs.CC] for this version)\nL24:    | 【12†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.1704.02086†doi.org】 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Michael A. Forbes [【13†view email】]\nL28: [v1] Fri, 7 Apr 2017 03:54:42 UTC (484 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled A Zero Knowledge Sumcheck and its Applications, by Alessandro Chiesa and 2 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * 【8†View PDF】\nL37:   * 【9†HTML (experimental)】\nL38:   * 【14†TeX Source】\nL39: \nL40: 【15†view license】\nL41: \nL42: ### Additional Features\nL43: \nL44:   * 【16†Audio Summary】\nL45: ### Current browse context:\nL46: \nL47: cs.CC\nL48: \nL49: 【17†< prev】   |   【18†next >】\nL50: \nL51: 【19†new】 | 【20†recent】 | 【21†2017-04】\nL52: \nL53: Change to browse by:\nL54: \nL55: 【22†cs】\nL56: 【23†cs.CR】\nL57: \nL58: ### References & Citations\nL59: \nL60:   * 【24†SciX†scixplorer.org】\nL61:   * 【25†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com】\nL62:   * 【26†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org】\nL63: ### 【27†DBLP†dblp.uni-trier.de】 - CS Bibliography\nL64: \nL65: 【28†listing†dblp.uni-trier.de】 | 【29†bibtex†dblp.uni-trier.de】\nL66: \nL67: 【30†Alessandro Chiesa†dblp.uni-trier.de】\nL68: 【31†Michael A. Forbes†dblp.uni-trier.de】\nL69: 【32†Nicholas Spooner†dblp.uni-trier.de】\nL70: \nL71: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL72: \nL73: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL74: \nL75: [Button: ×]\nL76: \nL77: loading...\nL78: \nL79: Data provided by:\nL80: \nL81: ### Bookmark\nL82: \nL83: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL84: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL85: \nL86: [In